WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.399
Film Courage: Once the screenwriter finishes
a script, what are they doing with it?

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:06.939
Carole Kirschner, Author: They are keeping
it in a drawer and they’re just waiting

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:08.189
for somebody to come along.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:09.189
That’s what…no…

00:00:09.189 --> 00:00:10.189
Film Courage: And they are Tweeting about
it.

00:00:10.189 --> 00:00:13.809
Carole Kirschner: They are Tweeting about
it “I’ve got a great script.”

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:18.891
Well, what you want to do is you want to get
it out into the world absolutely and there

00:00:18.891 --> 00:00:21.730
is a couple of ways to do that.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:28.380
One is to send it to somebody, to give it
to somebody who can give it to somebody somebody

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:34.410
else, who can give it to somebody who is in
a position to evaluate it or possibly hire

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.470
you or represent you.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:42.820
I’ve told this story many times about Madonna’s
manicurist.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.350
She goes on the road with Madonna but when
she wasn’t on the road, she had a salon,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:52.160
she worked at a salon and there was a woman
who came in and was her customer (her regular

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.440
customer) and this woman had a son who was
a screenwriter and she would tell the manicurist

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.840
“My son!

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.550
He’s so talented.”

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.570
And then one day she said “Would you show
his script to Madonna?”

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.830
And no…”Would you read his script?”

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.680
And the manicurist said “Sure.”

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.920
Everybody thinks they know how to read a script.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.270
So she read it and said “You know what?

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:15.640
This is really good.”

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.240
And so the mom said “Would you show it to
Madonna?”

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.720
And the manicurist said “Yeah…”

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.390
Now the manicurist never gave it to Madonna,
she gave it to Madonna’s assistant.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:32.710
But the assistant read it and thought it was
good and then she actually handed it to Madonna.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:37.390
Madonna eventually (this whole thing took
like a year), she read it, she thought it

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.550
was good and she gave it to her agent and
the agent thought it was good, brought the

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.080
guy in and he got representation.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.870
So you never know.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:52.720
You know, a friend of a friend of a friend
(somebody who can get it to a junior agent,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.390
a junior manager, a junior executive).

00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:00.479
Or get it higher up if you know somebody who
knows somebody, so you want to launch it into

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:01.479
the world.

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:08.810
You also want to absolutely enter it into
contests but don’t spend your money on Shmegeggie

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:18.260
screenplay contests, only do the top five
or six prestigious ones because the truth

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:26.110
is the community is not looking at the (I
don’t mean to insult anybody) certain screenplay

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:29.180
writing contest (most likely they’re not).

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.810
Film Courage: How would you know?

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.410
Is there a site or how would you know what
are the top contests?

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:38.060
Carole Kirschner: Yeah, Google ‘Top Screenwriting
Contests.’

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:42.610
You know, it’s certainly going to be Nichol’s,
it’s going to be Page [The Page Awards],

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:54.770
it’s going to be Austin [Austin Film Festival],
there’s Final Draft, there’s others.

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:59.530
Definitely go online and just Google ‘Top’
ones.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:02.320
There’s lots of sites that talk about the
best ones.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.849
The ones I just mentioned are well-known and
well-received.

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:12.270
In television there’s The Tracking-Board,
which is really good.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.970
That’s the thing that Mickey Fisher entered
his on Tracking B [The Tracking-Board].

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:16.970
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.599
And that’s the name of the site, The Tracking-Board?

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:19.180
Carole Kirschner: Yes.

